ing  for example the word for edible in  galician  and in portuguese another difference  in portuguese s is pronounced z between  vowels  but not in galician for example the word  for house  in galician and in portuguese  the word for thing in galician  and in portuguese causa  in galician c represents a voiceless  dental fricative  before i and e as in spanish for example  the word for five  in galician cinco and in portuguese  another one in galician the digraph ch  is pronounced in portuguese it's  for example the verb meaning to call in  galician  and in portuguese another difference  the galician rhotic consonant is like  the spanish one  a trill or a tap there is no guttural  rhodic sound like in portuguese  for example the word meaning rich in  galician  and in portuguese in galician there are  no phonemic nasal vowels  there are vowels that become nasalized  due to the surrounding environment  in other words they're next to a nasal  consonant but there are no pairs of  words that are distinguished by vowel  nasality  and vowel nasality is not a part of  galician orthography  the word for bread in galician and in  portuguese  here you can see that the nasal vowel  that existed in galician portuguese has  been lost in galatian  but remains in portuguese in galician  there's a nasal consonant instead  note that when n comes at the end of a  word in galician it's pronounced  rather than n also notice the loss of  the diphthong  the word for brother in galatians  and in portuguese is the standard form  and used in western dialects  while irmo is used in central and  eastern dialects  iramang seems more similar to castilian  hermano  judging by its nasal consonant and lack  of a diphthong  irumau retains the diphthong that is  also found in portuguese  but when there's a diphthong in galician  there's no nasal whether a vowel or a  consonant  we can see this in numerous words such  as the word for  hand and the plural form hands in  galician  mang mans mao  mouse 